javadrills/13-forced-interleavings/01-lo.explanation:
Let the map do the read and the write in one atomic step:

```java
static void increment(ConcurrentMap<String, Integer> counts, String key) {
    counts.merge(key, 1, Integer::sum);
}
```

`compute(key, (k, v) -> v == null ? 1 : v + 1)` is equally right. The forced-interleaving test catches the plausible wrong counters deterministically: `Integer v = counts.get(key); counts.put(key, v == null ? 1 : v + 1)` reads `null` on thread A, the test thread writes 1, A overwrites it with 1, and the test sees 1 instead of 2 on every run. `getOrDefault` instead of `get` fails the same way, because it reads through `get`. The 4-thread stress test is the kind of test a race usually gets; it catches the buggy counter only on some runs, which is why the forced test exists.

