javadrills/13-forced-interleavings/05-a-.explanation:
Wait with a bound, and cancel on every path that stops waiting:

```java
Future<T> f = pool.submit(work);
try {
    return f.get(timeoutMs, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
} catch (TimeoutException late) {
    f.cancel(true);
    return fallback;
} catch (ExecutionException failed) {
    if (failed.getCause() instanceof RuntimeException r) throw r;
    throw new IllegalStateException(failed.getCause());
} catch (InterruptedException e) {
    f.cancel(true);
    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
    return fallback;
}
```

The tests catch the plausible wrong waits. Returning the fallback on timeout without `cancel(true)` leaves the lookup running, and the late-task test sees it start and never stop. `cancel(false)` is not enough either: it only stops a task that has not started yet, and in the interrupted-caller test the task has certainly started, so it keeps running there. Rethrowing the `ExecutionException` itself, or wrapping every failure, hands the caller the wrong type (the unwrapping test expects the task's own `IllegalArgumentException`).

